Rather than stopping at the first split, this code records both root-to-value paths. Walking through the matching part gives the lowest shared ancestor. It is a little longer than the direct BST search, but the steps are easy to inspect.

The tree is not modified. If a target is not found, the method returns `nullptr`.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) : data(val), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
private:
    bool findPath(TreeNode* node, int target, std::vector<TreeNode*>& path) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return false;
        }

        path.push_back(node);
        if (node->data == target) {
            return true;
        }

        if (findPath(node->left, target, path) ||
            findPath(node->right, target, path)) {
            return true;
        }

        path.pop_back();
        return false;
    }

public:
    TreeNode* lca(TreeNode* root, int p, int q) {
        std::vector<TreeNode*> firstPath;
        std::vector<TreeNode*> secondPath;

        if (!findPath(root, p, firstPath) ||
            !findPath(root, q, secondPath)) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        TreeNode* answer = nullptr;
        size_t i = 0;
        while (i < firstPath.size() && i < secondPath.size() &&
               firstPath[i] == secondPath[i]) {
            answer = firstPath[i];
            ++i;
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the paths and recursive calls.

This is the general binary tree version. It checks both subtrees and reports a node when one target is found below each side. The BST property is not needed here, so it does more work than a direct ordered search.

The important edge cases are a null root and a target equal to the current node. The usual problem assumption is that both target values exist in the tree.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null; right = null; }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public TreeNode lca(TreeNode root, int p, int q) {
        if (root == null || root.data == p || root.data == q) {
            return root;
        }

        TreeNode leftResult = lca(root.left, p, q);
        TreeNode rightResult = lca(root.right, p, q);

        if (leftResult != null && rightResult != null) {
            return root;
        }

        return leftResult != null ? leftResult : rightResult;
    }
}
```

For a tree rooted at 8, if one value is found under 3 and the other under 10, both recursive calls return a node, so 8 becomes the answer.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since both subtrees may be visited.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the recursion stack.

At each node, the two target values can only be in the left subtree, the right subtree, or on different sides. The third case means the current node is the answer.

This version uses recursion, so a very unbalanced tree can create a deeper call stack. It also handles `p == q` by returning that node.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null; right = null; }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public TreeNode lca(TreeNode root, int p, int q) {
        if (root == null) {
            return null;
        }

        if (p < root.data && q < root.data) {
            return lca(root.left, p, q);
        }

        if (p > root.data && q > root.data) {
            return lca(root.right, p, q);
        }

        return root;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(h)` because only one branch is followed.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the recursive calls.

The BST ordering makes the search short. If both values are smaller than the current node, move left. If both are larger, move right. Otherwise, the current node is where their paths split.

The input tree is only read, not changed. An empty tree returns `nullptr`.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) : data(val), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
public:
    TreeNode* lca(TreeNode* root, int p, int q) {
        TreeNode* current = root;

        while (current != nullptr) {
            if (p < current->data && q < current->data) {
                current = current->left;
            } else if (p > current->data && q > current->data) {
                current = current->right;
            } else {
                return current;
            }
        }

        return nullptr;
    }
};
```

For example, with values 5, 2, and 8, searching for 2 and 8 stops at 5 because one value is on each side.

Time complexity: `O(h)`, where `h` is the tree height.
Space complexity: `O(1)`, excluding the tree itself.

